  "notes": "New York's live-instrument R&B producer. `confidence` is **low** — the research rates the entry medium-low, sourcing the credits and the keyboard-first identity but marking the parametric detail as inference. The stated habit is a contradiction and it is what the numbers hold: \"a live-sounding kit inside a radio mix — a played or convincingly-humanised kick/snare with the backbeat on 2&4, ghost 16ths, brushes on the ballads, and a hand-percussion layer, all sitting in a modern loud master\". So the snare carries a 3–10 ms displacement and a 0.54 ghost probability — live behaviour — while the velocity tiers are the loud, compressed modern-master values rather than the soft tiers the rest of the played-kit models here use. He is one of the seven models in this section with an 808, because the entry gives him both: \"played electric bass with walking motion on the retro-facing cuts, and a sustained sub-808 on the contemporary ones. This dual capability is why he works across this section and `trap-soul`.\" `drums.bass808` is therefore role **counter_riff** with a near-zero slide probability, which keeps the sub while leaving the walking bass generating — role `bassline` would have deleted the retro half of his work. Harmony is \"gospel-jazz — minor cycles with real cadences, secondary dominants, diminished approach chords, and a whole-step modulation for the last chorus. He is a keyboard player, so the harmony is played rather than looped\", which is `susOrDimProb` for the approach chords and a harmonic rhythm that moves every bar. The arrangement is \"a full song form rather than a loop\". ⚠ Every drum number, the 808 values and all melody figures here are INFERRED. ⚠ The half-time variant the entry names (snare on 3 with an 808 kick under it) is not encoded — one model states one placement.",
